Compatibility scope
-------------------
This is the official pure core (v2) engine present in release 1.18.30.
The release also contains packages/opencode/src/patch/index.ts (legacy v1).
These are NOT identical: the legacy parser silently skips malformed lines
and its EOF-marker branch is unreachable. The core parser validates bodies
and supports EOF markers. The core matcher also appends pure additions after
an existing final blank line, where the legacy matcher inserts before it.
This vendor deliberately uses the core implementation
without recreating the legacy bugs, and does not claim byte-for-byte legacy
CLI behavior. @opencode-ai/core is private:true at this commit; the installed
@opencode-ai/plugin@1.18.29 exposes no injectable patch parser/matcher API.

The adapter ../patch.ts adds read-only, versioned filesystem planning, input
limits, and a sequential in-memory overlay. It uses the native apply_patch
tool's add-file final-newline rule: empty stays empty, otherwise append LF
only if content does not already end with LF. derive/joinBom are used exactly
as upstream for updates. In particular, there is no CRLF normalization: CRs
in unchanged file lines and patch content remain, and updates may produce
mixed endings just as upstream does. Upstream EOF matching prefers the end
at each matching tolerance, then falls back to forward search. Upstream
permits surrounding text/heredocs around the first Begin/End envelope.

Move overwrite behavior was verified in the same release's legacy tool:
https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/3104c1428ec91f809e5ab86631300de41eb6952e/packages/opencode/src/tool/apply_patch.ts
Its move branch writes the destination with writeWithDirs, then removes
the source; it does not reject an existing destination. Adds also overwrite.
The core tool currently rejects moves, although its pure parser supports
them. The remote planner implements the verified legacy move/overwrite
behavior with both source and destination initial snapshot versions, and
rejects self-moves after resolution to avoid deleting the result. Repeated
paths compose in patch order (not as simultaneous renames). File/descendant
target combinations are rejected. Absent-to-absent net changes are omitted.
Callers must verify ALL returned versions before writing any changes.

Planner limits: 10 MiB UTF-8 patch text, 10 MiB total initial snapshot content,
10 MiB total overlay content, 50,000 lines per input/output text, and 1,024
distinct resolved paths. Line limits also bound the upstream spread/splice
argument count without changing its implementation. These are adapter
resource limits, not additions to the official patch language.

